  "summary": "A press release from BlurMe Inc. claims a 260% surge in demand for its AI license-plate redaction software, citing the California Bartholomew v. Parking Concepts ruling imposing $2,500-per-plate penalties and a wave of Flock Safety-related class actions. It references unauthorized ICE/out-of-state searches of Flock databases in San Francisco, San Jose, Mountain View, Ventura County, and Oxnard, and the California AG's action against El Cajon, to market its product.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "any entity operating an ALPR camera without a compliant privacy policy owes at least $2,500 per scanned plate \u2014 even without proof of data misuse",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "San Francisco's Flock Safety camera database had been searched over 1.6 million times by out-of-state and federal agencies \u2014 including ICE \u2014 over seven months",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "At least 30 U.S. cities have canceled or suspended ALPR camera contracts since January 2025",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    }
